 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §stable-tab-keys — superseded 2026-10-23: closing a tab renumbers the others to `t1..tn`
+- Since: 2026-10-23 (maintainer override of the 2026-09-02 rule quoted under "Superseded" below)
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs, in their current order, to contiguous keys `t1..tn` and sets
+  `next` to n + 1, so the next tab opened gets `t(n+1)`. Closing `t2` of `t1,t2,t3` leaves `t1,t2`.
+- Forbidden: leaving a gap after a close (`t1,t3`), or opening a tab with a key from a `next` that ran past `tn`.
+- Reason: the maintainer overrode the 2026-09-02 rule on 2026-10-23. No further rationale was recorded.
+- Accepted cost: the key is still the React key and the URL state. A tab strip keyed on `key` remounts every tab to the
+  right of the closed one (the 2026-08-29 incident), and a URL saved before a close can name a different tab after it.
+- Superseded (2026-09-02 to 2026-10-23): "a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a
+  tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`)." Renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous was forbidden.
+  Its reason: "the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed
+  one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys"
+  test)."
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` (the "close keeps keys" test is now "close renumbers keys")
